------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presentations of groups
--
-- Amy, Ross and Wesley's 3-qubit Toffoli-Hadamard presentation as
-- circuits (Circuit.Base): the gates X, H, CX, K, CCX, NK and CCZ, and
-- the relations of Figures 4, 7 and 8 with the definition of K
--
-- The paper's alphabet (Syntactics) has a letter for each gate on each
-- qubit and orientation (X₀, X₁, X₂, CX₀₁, CX₁₀, CX₀₂, …), most of them
-- defined by the first relations of Figure 4 from X₀, CX₀₁, CCX₁₂ and
-- K₁₂.  Here the gates are X, H on one wire, CX (control on the upper
-- wire) and K = H ⊗ H on two, CCX (target on the top wire), NK =
-- K_[0,1,2,3] (H ⊗ H on the lower wires when the top wire is 0) and CCZ
-- on three; qubit 2, the last tensor factor, is wire 0, and qubit 0 is
-- wire 2.  Each of the paper's letters is a circuit of the same name:
-- the gates placed by _↑ (X₀ = X ↑ ↑, CX₀₁ = CX ↑, …), and the letters
-- Figure 4 defines by their definitions, which therefore hold by
-- definition and are not rules here.  The other relations are rules,
-- word for word, except the four commutations of gates on disjoint
-- wires ((26), (27), (102), (103)), which are structural, and one rule
-- is added: H on wire 1 is K H, as the paper's H₁ is K₁₂ H₂ (and H on
-- wire 2, the paper's H₀ = K₀₁ K₁₂ H₂, follows by cong↑).  Every rule
-- but that one is stated on three wires.  Circuits.Iso shows the two
-- presentations isomorphic on three wires, and Circuits.Presentation
-- carries the main theorem over.
--
-- Generated by tools/gen_circuits.py from Syntactics.
------------------------------------------------------------------------

